  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings and synthesized evidence are heavily concentrated on the UK context, which may limit their direct applicability to countries with different transport policies and urban designs.",
      "what": "This review synthesizes literature across transport planning, disability studies, urban design, psychology, policy, and assistive technology to analyze how legal frameworks, built environments, and emerging technologies affect the travel experiences of visually impaired people. Drawing primarily on UK evidence, it maps legacy and contemporary barriers in infrastructure, information systems, and evolving street designs that restrict independent mobility.",
      "why": "It establishes a multi-disciplinary research and design agenda by connecting physical infrastructure failures to overlooked psychological and emotional dimensions of travel, such as spatial cognition and social participation."
